1. Why Is It Called "Bayes"?
It is named after Thomas Bayes, an 18th-century English Presbyterian minister and mathematician.

Bayes was trying to solve an inverse thinking problem:

Usually, if you know the cause, it's easy to predict the symptoms. (e.g., If you have a severe flu, you expect a high fever.)

But doctors, detectives, and engineers always work backwards: You see the symptom first; what caused it?

His rule—Bayes' Theorem—is simply a mathematical formula for flipping conditional probability around:   
