In 1761, £100 was a substantial sum of money—far more than a simple tip for sorting out an estate.

Depending on how economic historians measure purchasing power, that £100 reflects vastly different values:

In Everyday Consumer Goods (CPI / Retail Purchasing Power):
Adjusted strictly for inflation of basic staples (bread, ale, candles, fabric), £100 in 1761 equates to approximately £18,000 to £25,000 today (around $23,000 to $32,000 USD).

In Real Economic Terms (Wages & Status Value):
Looking at what £100 represented relative to ordinary earnings reveals its true weight:

In 1761, a skilled craftsman or stonemason earned roughly £30 to £40 a year. An ordinary farm laborer might earn £15 to £20 a year.

A £100 bequest represented roughly two and a half to three full years of wages for a skilled artisan, or five to six years of labor for an unskilled worker.

In terms of contemporary labor share and economic status, that equates to roughly £150,000 to £250,000 today.

For a modest, independent Dissenting minister like Richard Price—who lived prudently and wasn't drawing an opulent Anglican church income—Bayes’ bequest was a meaningful windfall.

Price proved worthy of the trust. Instead of taking the cash and tucking the papers into an attic trunk, he recognized the genius in Bayes’ draft, devoted two years of his own intellectual labor to working through the probability integrals, and presented it to the Royal Society.

Price went on to become an extraordinary figure in his own right: he essentially founded modern actuarial life insurance mathematics (the Equitable Life assurance society), advised Benjamin Franklin and the American revolutionaries on public debt and finance, and corresponded with Thomas Jefferson and John Adams. He was a champion of liberty and an honest steward of a friend's legacy.

